t it ran and some of the  information about the vectors and the  classes and there are two support  vectors so now let's find those two  support vectors on our graph and this  might be a little hard to see the orange  outline around this point and this point  indicate that those are the two vectors  that will support this model and thus we  call them the support vectors now let's  get the parameters of the hyperplane  from the model and let's plot a line  that will be our hyperplane there's our  hyperplane line and therefore to the  left and below our mules and to the  right and above our horses above that  line let's take a few new observations  and I've encoded these so that it would  be illustrative well take these new  observations and we'll plot them and I'm  going to generate a new plot red being  the horses black dots being the mules  I'll create an X and y-axis I'm gonna  plot three dots and you can see one of  them the green dot the first one canna  falls near the mules the blue one kind  of falls near the horses and visually we  might conclude hate one's a mule one's a  horse but what about the orange dot well  for the orange dot we really need our  hyperplane we really need something to  clearly indicate which side it's on and  of course by looking  at this data visualization you can tell  that the orange is probably a mule and  let's verify those results by sticking  those observations into our model and  predict some outcomes and down below you  can see that for the three data points  one two and three the first one the  green one was negative 1 that's a mule  the second one was a positive one that's  blue it's the Blue Point which was a  horse and the third one which we weren't  sure about but clearly the SVM model  correctly predicted that that Third  Point would be a negative 1 would be a  mule and we can visually see that by the  hyperplane on the graph so that's a  quick example of SVM in general support  vector machines are binary classifiers  we don't use them for higher level  classification if you had three or more  classes you might want to use something  like random forests what applications  you can use it for face detection text  categorization image classification and  bioinformatics really anywhere that you  have the need to classify things into  two groups  so the key takeaways in this video we  learned about what machine learning is  and some of the different types of  machine learning algorithms supervised  and unsupervised and reinforcement  learning and we focused primarily on  supervised learning and support vector  machines understanding support vector  machines as a binary classifier and the  intuition between the problem and the  solution take the two closest points in  the two classes that maximize the margin  or the distance between them we looked  at what happens if your two classes are  not linearly separable an understanding  SVM kernel and how you could map a  non-linear separable problem to a higher  dimension and thereby creating a clear  separation and then we looked at the use  case of horses and mules it's a small  data set but very indicative of how SVM  works and then the applications of  support vector machine and although we  only covered for face detection text  categorization image classification and  bioinformatics there are literally  thousands and thousands of applications  of SVM  and that's it if you'd like the code  that we use in this example or you have  any questions or concerns please post  them in the comments we have a great  library of these videos up on our  YouTube site and we hope you subscribe  to it and watch them all they're very  helpful especially to beginner data  scientists thanks again this is Pete  Ferrari and happy data sciencing hi  there if you like this video subscribe  to the simple learn YouTube channel and  click here to watch similar videos de  nerd up and get certified click here
 

